🌱 1️⃣ What is “State” in Compose?

👉 State = Data that can change over time
…and when it changes, the UI automatically updates (recomposes).

Example:

var count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }


Here, count is a state variable — when it changes, Compose redraws the screen.

So:

State = the “memory” of your UI.

🧩 2️⃣ What is a Stateful Composable?

A Stateful composable = a composable function that owns and manages its own state inside it.

Example:
@Composable
fun CounterExample() {
    var count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }  // State is INSIDE
    Button(onClick = { count++ }) {
        Text("Count: $count")
    }
}

💬 Meaning:

count is remembered inside this function.

When you click the button → count increases → Compose rebuilds the UI → the Text updates.

✅ Simple. Works perfectly fine for small apps.

🧠 But… Problem?

What if you want to reuse this CounterExample() somewhere else
but manage its value from outside (like from parent screen)?

You can’t — because its state is trapped inside (not reusable).
That’s why we use the concept called 👇

🏗️ 3️⃣ State Hoisting

State Hoisting = Moving (lifting) the state up from a child composable to its parent composable.

Why?

To make your composable reusable and testable.

To let parent control the state, instead of child controlling it.

🔹 “Hoisting” literally means “lifting up”.

Like in real life:

If a child can’t carry a heavy bag, the parent lifts (hoists) it.

Same idea here:

Child Composable: Should display the UI and take actions (but not store data permanently).

Parent Composable: Should hold the actual state.

⚙️ Example 1 — Stateful Version
@Composable
fun CounterStateful() {
    var count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) } // inside child
    CounterDisplay(count = count, onIncrement = { count++ })
}

@Composable
fun CounterDisplay(count: Int, onIncrement: () -> Unit) {
    Button(onClick = onIncrement) {
        Text("Clicked $count times")
    }
}


✅ Works, but state is inside CounterStateful().

⚙️ Example 2 — Stateless (State Hoisted) Version

Now, let’s move (hoist) the state outside 👇

@Composable
fun CounterStateless(count: Int, onIncrement: () -> Unit) {
    Button(onClick = onIncrement) {
        Text("Clicked $count times")
    }
}

@Composable
fun CounterHost() { // parent
    var count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) } // state here (lifted)
    CounterStateless(
        count = count,
        onIncrement = { count++ } // pass callback
    )
}

💬 Meaning:

CounterStateless() just shows the UI.

CounterHost() holds and manages the state.

We hoisted the state from child → parent.

🔁 4️⃣ Difference Summary
Concept	Meaning	State Location	Reusability
Stateful Composable	Composable that manages its own state	Inside the composable	Low
Stateless Composable	Composable that receives state & actions from outside	In parent	High
State Hoisting	Moving state from child → parent	In parent composable	Improves flexibility & control
🧩 5️⃣ Why State Hoisting is Important?
Reason	Description
✅ Reusability	You can use the same UI with different data.
🧪 Testability	Easier to test since logic & UI are separate.
🔄 Single Source of Truth	One place controls data, avoids confusion.
🧱 Clean Architecture	UI (stateless) and logic (state) are clearly divided.
⚙️ 6️⃣ Real-Life Analogy

Imagine:

You built a light bulb composable.

It has a button to turn on/off.

If the bulb itself remembers the state (Stateful), you can’t control it from outside.

But if the bulb just shows its state (on/off) and takes commands (like “toggle”),
then someone else (the switch board = parent) can control it.

That’s state hoisting — parent controlling the state, child just reacting.

🧩 7️⃣ How to Convert Stateful → Stateless (Step-by-Step)

Let’s convert your component manually:

Step	What to Do	Example
1️⃣	Identify the state inside composable	var text by remember { mutableStateOf("") }
2️⃣	Move it to parent	Put same line in parent composable
3️⃣	Pass value as parameter	TextField(value = text, ...)
4️⃣	Pass callback to modify state	onValueChange = { text = it }
5️⃣	Remove state from child	No remember inside child

✅ Done — now your child is stateless!

📘 Quick Example: TextField Hoisting

Before (Stateful):

@Composable
fun NameInput() {
    var name by remember { mutableStateOf("") }
    TextField(value = name, onValueChange = { name = it })
}


After (Stateless with Hoisting):

@Composable
fun NameInput(value: String, onValueChange: (String) -> Unit) {
    TextField(value = value, onValueChange = onValueChange)
}

@Composable
fun NameScreen() {
    var name by remember { mutableStateOf("") }
    NameInput(value = name, onValueChange = { name = it })
}


Now, NameInput() is fully stateless and reusable in multiple places ✅

💡 Easy Formula to Remember
STATEFUL = owns the state (remember {...})
STATELESS = uses the state (value, onChange)
STATE HOISTING = move state to parent (lift it up)

🎯 In short (Never forget summary):
Term	What it means	Example summary
Stateful	Composable controls its own state	remember { mutableStateOf(...) } inside composable
Stateless	Composable gets state + callback from parent	value and onValueChange passed as parameters
State Hoisting	Moving state up to parent for control	Parent holds state, child shows UI